1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,839
Five friends want one
photo, standing in a row.

2
00:00:04,239 --> 00:00:05,388
Who stands first?

3
00:00:05,388 --> 00:00:06,582
Who stands second?

4
00:00:06,582 --> 00:00:08,575
Every new order is a new photo.

5
00:00:09,025 --> 00:00:11,492
Aarav offered to take
every possible photo.

6
00:00:11,842 --> 00:00:14,103
He thought it would take a few minutes.

7
00:00:14,103 --> 00:00:16,314
It takes one hundred and twenty photos!

8
00:00:16,314 --> 00:00:17,214
Why so many?

9
00:00:17,683 --> 00:00:20,009
The answer hides in a long product.

10
00:00:20,009 --> 00:00:22,593
And maths has a short way to write it.

11
00:00:22,593 --> 00:00:23,493
Factorial.

12
00:00:24,022 --> 00:00:27,012
Let us count with the
counting rule from last time.

13
00:00:27,012 --> 00:00:29,009
There are five spots in the row.

14
00:00:29,409 --> 00:00:32,208
The first spot can take
any of the five friends.

15
00:00:32,608 --> 00:00:34,712
The second spot has four left.

16
00:00:34,712 --> 00:00:35,629
Then three.

17
00:00:35,629 --> 00:00:36,529
Then two.

18
00:00:36,447 --> 00:00:37,573
Then just one.

19
00:00:37,973 --> 00:00:41,133
Five times four times
three times two times one.

20
00:00:41,133 --> 00:00:43,164
One hundred and twenty photos.

21
00:00:43,814 --> 00:00:46,397
Now watch the count grow
as the row gets longer.

22
00:00:46,797 --> 00:00:48,312
One friend: one way.

23
00:00:48,312 --> 00:00:49,922
Two friends: two ways.

24
00:00:50,322 --> 00:00:52,031
Three friends: six ways.

25
00:00:52,031 --> 00:00:53,837
Four friends: twenty four.

26
00:00:54,237 --> 00:00:56,402
Five friends: one hundred and twenty.

27
00:00:56,402 --> 00:00:57,302
Ten friends?

28
00:00:57,222 --> 00:00:59,269
More than three and a half million.

29
00:00:59,669 --> 00:01:03,794
The numbers grow so fast that this
product needs its own short name.

30
00:01:04,444 --> 00:01:05,820
NCERT gives it one.

31
00:01:05,820 --> 00:01:09,108
n factorial, written as n
with an exclamation mark.

32
00:01:09,508 --> 00:01:13,041
It means the product of the
first n natural numbers.

33
00:01:13,041 --> 00:01:16,481
One times two times three,
all the way up to n.

34
00:01:17,231 --> 00:01:21,992
So five factorial is one times two
times three times four times five.

35
00:01:21,992 --> 00:01:23,735
One hundred and twenty.

36
00:01:24,485 --> 00:01:26,915
Now the trick that makes it powerful.

37
00:01:26,915 --> 00:01:29,683
Five factorial is five
times four factorial.

38
00:01:30,433 --> 00:01:34,825
In general, n factorial is n
times n minus one factorial.

39
00:01:34,825 --> 00:01:39,615
Each factorial holds the one
before it, like boxes inside boxes.

40
00:01:40,365 --> 00:01:42,426
But what about zero factorial?

41
00:01:42,426 --> 00:01:44,436
There is nothing to multiply.

42
00:01:44,836 --> 00:01:47,143
NCERT defines zero factorial as one.

43
00:01:47,143 --> 00:01:50,560
Think of it as the number
of ways to line up nobody.

44
00:01:50,560 --> 00:01:53,068
There is exactly one way: do nothing.

45
00:01:53,668 --> 00:01:55,974
It also keeps the rule working.

46
00:01:55,974 --> 00:01:58,930
One factorial is one times zero factorial.

47
00:01:58,930 --> 00:02:01,897
That only works if zero factorial is one.

48
00:02:02,547 --> 00:02:03,645
Side by side.

49
00:02:03,645 --> 00:02:06,250
Five minus three, then the factorial.

50
00:02:06,250 --> 00:02:07,867
That is two factorial.

51
00:02:07,867 --> 00:02:08,767
Just two.

52
00:02:09,101 --> 00:02:11,856
Now five factorial minus three factorial.

53
00:02:11,856 --> 00:02:14,196
One hundred and twenty minus six.

54
00:02:14,196 --> 00:02:15,998
One hundred and fourteen.

55
00:02:15,998 --> 00:02:18,111
The brackets change everything.

56
00:02:18,711 --> 00:02:19,646
Your turn.

57
00:02:19,646 --> 00:02:24,224
Is six factorial divided by three
factorial equal to two factorial?

58
00:02:24,224 --> 00:02:25,600
Pause and decide.

59
00:02:28,800 --> 00:02:29,700
No!

60
00:02:29,297 --> 00:02:33,733
Six factorial over three factorial
is six times five times four.

61
00:02:33,733 --> 00:02:35,503
One hundred and twenty.

62
00:02:35,503 --> 00:02:38,159
You cannot divide the numbers inside.

63
00:02:38,808 --> 00:02:39,810
Remember this.

64
00:02:39,810 --> 00:02:43,424
n factorial multiplies every
whole number from one up to n.

65
00:02:43,424 --> 00:02:45,156
And zero factorial is one.

66
00:02:45,856 --> 00:02:48,031
Now, a worked example from the NCERT book.

67
00:02:48,381 --> 00:02:53,931
Find x, if one over eight factorial,
plus one over nine factorial, equals

68
00:02:53,931 --> 00:02:55,719
x over ten factorial.

69
00:02:56,219 --> 00:02:57,119
Step one.

70
00:02:57,076 --> 00:03:00,232
Write the big factorials
using eight factorial.

71
00:03:00,232 --> 00:03:03,283
Nine factorial is nine
times eight factorial.

72
00:03:03,283 --> 00:03:06,929
Ten factorial is ten times
nine times eight factorial.

73
00:03:07,679 --> 00:03:08,579
Step two.

74
00:03:08,548 --> 00:03:11,154
Multiply every term by ten factorial.

75
00:03:11,154 --> 00:03:14,248
Ten factorial over eight
factorial is ninety.

76
00:03:14,248 --> 00:03:17,129
Ten factorial over nine factorial is ten.

77
00:03:17,879 --> 00:03:18,821
Step three.

78
00:03:18,821 --> 00:03:20,877
So ninety plus ten equals x.

79
00:03:21,627 --> 00:03:23,069
x is one hundred.

80
00:03:23,719 --> 00:03:25,180
Now, the exam trap.

81
00:03:25,530 --> 00:03:28,674
Many students write zero
factorial equals zero.

82
00:03:28,674 --> 00:03:32,734
Then five factorial over zero
factorial looks like it breaks.

83
00:03:33,134 --> 00:03:36,200
But zero factorial is one, by definition.

84
00:03:36,200 --> 00:03:41,114
So five factorial over zero factorial
is simply one hundred and twenty.

85
00:03:41,764 --> 00:03:43,351
Where is this asked?

86
00:03:43,351 --> 00:03:48,505
We found no past paper question on
just this notation, so no chip lights.

87
00:03:48,505 --> 00:03:54,032
The type: simplify a ratio of factorials,
or find n from a factorial equation.

88
00:03:54,632 --> 00:03:55,909
Exam level now.

89
00:03:55,909 --> 00:03:56,809
Find n.

90
00:03:56,689 --> 00:03:58,021
Pause and solve.

91
00:04:03,421 --> 00:04:04,920
The answer is C.

92
00:04:04,920 --> 00:04:05,820
Seven.

93
00:04:06,129 --> 00:04:09,573
Expand the bigger factorial
down to n factorial.

94
00:04:09,573 --> 00:04:15,020
n plus two factorial is n plus two,
times n plus one, times n factorial.

95
00:04:15,520 --> 00:04:16,972
Cancel n factorial.

96
00:04:16,972 --> 00:04:20,874
So n plus two, times n plus
one, equals seventy two.

97
00:04:21,374 --> 00:04:25,330
Seventy two is nine times
eight, two numbers in a row.

98
00:04:25,330 --> 00:04:28,369
So n plus two is nine, and n is seven.

99
00:04:28,969 --> 00:04:30,509
Here is your recap.

100
00:04:30,509 --> 00:04:32,092
Screenshot this card.

101
00:04:35,292 --> 00:04:37,478
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

102
00:04:37,878 --> 00:04:38,778
One.

103
00:04:38,333 --> 00:04:40,165
What does five factorial mean?

104
00:04:41,065 --> 00:04:41,965
Two.

105
00:04:41,540 --> 00:04:43,796
Why is zero factorial equal to one?

106
00:04:44,696 --> 00:04:45,596
Three.

107
00:04:45,345 --> 00:04:48,385
Simplify seven factorial
over five factorial.

108
00:04:49,485 --> 00:04:53,042
Next time: choose a captain and
a vice captain from your team.

109
00:04:53,042 --> 00:04:53,955
Order matters.

110
00:04:53,955 --> 00:04:56,710
We count it with permutations
of distinct objects.
